s once filled with excitement  nothing is more thrilling to me than the  process of scientific discovery when the  wonder of nature is revealed one is less  breathless and awestruck imagine the joy  and intellectual satisfaction when the  constant expansion of our vast universe  was discovered when the complex nature  of the chemical bond was understood or  when the double helical structure of DNA  was solved imagine the excitement that  must have pervaded this campus when the  synthesis of DNA was achieved from an  RNA template imagine as well the fruits  of science the sense of accomplishment  in those engineers when an electrical  current was transformed into light when  sound waves were transmitted across long  distances in a while or when a cushion  of air was harvested to send men soaring  into the sky allow me the more modest  but personal anecdote beginning in 91 my  colleagues and I had the privilege of  working with structural biologists and  medicinal chemists  to test small chemicals that my  intercalate into the catalytic site of  the HIV protease and enzyme essential  for the production of infectious progeny  virus so overwhelming was the excitement  that overtook us when substances were  found to potently inhibit this enzyme  thereby blocking viral replication in  the test tube three years later we again  had the privilege of being the first to  administer one of these chemicals to  infected patients unmatch where the joy  and amazement as we watch the level of  HIV fall ever so dramatically at first  little did we know that we were sitting  on top of a fundamental discovery in  AIDS research but shortly thereafter by  simply asking why does the virus fall  and why does it fall in that manner it  quickly donnas that HIV must be turning  over rapidly in a very dynamic  equilibrium with a host using data from  patients working with mathematicians we  proved HIV replication in the patient  was rapid and remorseless in the course  of only a few weeks the old paradigm  that HIV was largely a latent virus was  completely shattered so incredible was  the ensuing intellectual satisfaction  that I now fully appreciate the meaning  of the line in a book the ascendant man  it reads when the answers so simple then  you hear God thinking despite the  breakneck speed of scientific discovery  in the field AIDS patients already faced  a decade and a half of horror and  disappointment but because of science  there is hope in the past two years with  new knowledge and new  therapy it has become possible to  control HIV so effectively that the  virus is no longer detectable in the  infected person this dramatic attack is  associated with the substantial clinical  benefit to the patient for the first  time in this dreadful epidemic the tie  has began to turn against the virus  although a cure is still not in hand as  stated in Time magazine the worst fear  the one that seeded a decade with the  spear the foreboding sense that the AIDS  virus might be invincible has finally  been subdued after years of cursing the  darkness of AIDS a candle of hope has  been lit by science aids however is not  over worldwide most infected persons  cannot access the promising therapies  and much remains to be done in  controlling the spread of this epidemic  it is my deepest hope and that the  recent scientific advances will inspire  government academia and the private  sector to remain vigilant and to  redouble our efforts to bring an end to  this tragedy prevention is the ultimate  key to controlling the epidemic and  vaccine science must now take center  stage as was declared by President  Clinton last year recently my thoughts  have taken me on a number of  self-reflective journeys  let me humbly share a few were the  graduates in our experiments attempting  to eliminate HIV from an infected person  I've learned that success in research as  is the case in most endeavors requires  both decision making and a willingness  to take inform risks as so eloquently  stated by Harris Shapiro of Princeton an  excessive zeal to avoid all risk is in  in an acceptance of mediocrity and an  application of leadership graduates you  must take on the toughest challenge but  view it as the greatest opportunity for  every noble work is at first seemingly  impossible heed the wise words of Teddy  Roosevelt before the turn of the century  far better it is to dare mighty things  to win glorious triumphs even though  checkered by failure then to rank with  those poor spirits who neither and join  or suffer much because they live in the  grey Twilight that knows neither victory  nor defeat more and more I've also  reflected on the role of science in our  society I'm disturbed by the public's  indifference to what we do as scientists  and engineers as children we all began  with a real desk for science we we were  curious and provocative and insightful  questions blurted out constantly  however by early adulthood this  proclivity to science when the joy of  discovery have somehow dissipated in  most only to be replaced by science  phobia consequently it is not surprising  that our society has often stereotyped  unflatteringly  those scientists and engineers who carry  out incomprehensible lines of work  Edison Bell and the Wright brothers  would be today's nerds even though the  products of their ingenuity have made  our world better I'm particularly  saddened by the fact that many of my  personal heroes and science are virtual  unknowns to a typical citizen while  certain dubious characters by  universally recognized and often  glorified by our society a scientist and  engineer we must change this by learning  to present our work in both interesting  and understandable ways indeed it is our  responsibility to bring back that spark  the sense of wonder about nature that  lies within every citizen I'm equally  troubled by this society's lack of  commitment to research in basic science  the former president said in a campaign  speech why should we subsidize  intellectual curiosity this simple but  naive question speaks volumes of the  lack of the appreciation of the richness  that uninhibited fundamental research  has brought us Carl Sagan said it best  when he wrote Maxwell wasn't thinking a  radio radar and television when he first  scratched out the fundamental equations  of electromagnetism Newton wasn't  dreaming of space flights or  communication satellites when he first  understood the motion of the moon Rankin  was in contemplating medical diagnosis  when he investigated a penetrating  radiation so mysterious  he called it x-rays Curie wasn't  thinking of cancer therapy when she  painstakingly extracted my new amounts  radium from tons of pitch boy Fleming  wasn't planning on saving lives of  millions with antibiotics when he  noticed circle free of bacteria around a  growth of mold Watson and Crick weren't  imagining the cure of genetic diseases  when they puzzle over the x-ray  diffraction pattern of DNA these  discoveries and multitude of others that  grazed and characterized our time were  made  ultimately by scientists given the  opportunity to explore what in their  opinions were basic questions in nature  cutting off fundamental curiosity-driven  science is like eating the seed corn  George Washington said in 1790 there is  nothing which can better deserve our  patronage than the promotion of science  knowledge is in every country the surest  basis of public happiness graduates in  the best tradition of MIT always  maintain unwaveringly a deep commitment  to excellence as you enter a career in  science or engineering stay in the  forefront of your chosen field and never  permit the excellence of your work to be  compromised  believe in what you're doing as Max  Planck said over the entrance of the  gates to the temple of science written  the words he must have faith it is a  quality which the scientists cannot  dispense with I remember where the great  deal of fondness might mate from the  class of 74 just like you what a bunch  they are they were stay young at heart  but should you need to grow do not  suppress your individuality and insist  on conformity continue to let  imagination and creativity percolate  throughout your lives for every great  events in science has issued from a new  audacity of the imagination  said John Dewey I'll also reflect it on  the contribution of my heritage to my  career we're not for the profound Asian  respect for intellectual achievements  and scholarly endeavors a scientist I  might not be today more ever values of  drive dedication imprinted during early  childhood have continued to serve me  well in any culture  there's simply no substitute for hard  work  I've been an American for so long that I  often forget that I'm also an immigrant  from time to time I could still sense  the desire that burns in the belly of a  new immigrant the desire to carve out a  place in the new world in the land of  opportunities to this day I maintain an  underdog mentality that motivates me to  a higher level of work ethics let me  begin to close with a comment throughout  its history  America's continued to benefit from the  drive labor and creativity of immigrants  many in the field of science and  engineering  just look among the graduates this  morning thus today one prevailing view  that immigrants constitute a constant  drain on society is simply baseless  wrong and shameful particularly in this  nation of immigrants  graduates as future scientists and  engineers it is likely that you will on  occasions be underappreciated  under-recognized and more likely  underpaid by our society you'll have to  take solace and satisfaction in knowing  that your work has helped to build a  better safer healthier and richer world  a world with hope members of the class  of 98 it matters not whether you work on  numbers atoms molecules machines  organisms the earth or the cosmos  through science and technology any one  of you can cast a giant shadow on our  planet congratulations and don't forget  to thank your parents  you
 

